      id: 'eng.safety.lmra',
      title: 'The LMRA and stopping work',
      pre: [
        {
          q: 'What is an LMRA?',
          options: ['A short check by the crew at the job, just before starting', 'A yearly safety audit done by an outside certification body', 'A form the safety manager fills in every week', 'The risk assessment made for a new building'],
          answer: 'A short check by the crew at the job, just before starting',
          why: 'LMRA stands for last-minute risk analysis: the last check that catches what changed since the job was planned.',
        },
      ],
      teach: [
        'The LMRA, the last-minute risk analysis, is a short check at the workplace, just before starting, by the people who will do the work. It comes from VCA, the Dutch safety checklist and certificate for any contractor: a firm hired to work on someone else’s site.\n\nPlans and permits are made in an office. The LMRA catches what has changed since: a leaking valve, someone working above you, a different machine than on the work order, the sheet that says which job to do where.',
        'Typical questions: Do I know what to do and how? What can go wrong here and now? Are the measures from the work order and permit in place?\n\nDo I have the right tools and PPE? Are others around me affected? If an answer is no or unclear, you don’t start.\n\nA good LMRA usually takes one to three minutes and is spoken out loud. Many sites use a pocket card with the questions as a reminder.',
        'Stop when it is unsafe. Under article 29 of the Arbowet, employees may stop work when, in their reasonable judgement, there is serious danger so immediate that there is no time to wait for the inspectorate.\n\nThey keep their pay. They must tell their supervisor right away, and the stop is reported to the Netherlands Labour Authority as soon as possible. Many sites widen this into a stop-work right for everyone, including visitors and contractors. Stopping is not failing; carrying on when unsure is.',
        'The trap is the LMRA turning into a ticked form. A card signed in two seconds checks nothing.\n\nGood teams name the risks out loud together, and a supervisor sometimes asks: “What did you find?” A stop that is thanked, not punished, is what keeps people stopping.\n\nWhat you hear when it works: “Hang on, that valve wasn’t open this morning.”',
        'Experts argue about the card. Some sites keep it as proof for audits; others drop it, because a signed card can replace the conversation it was meant to trigger. Either way, the test is whether jobs are ever stopped after an LMRA.\n\nWatch time pressure too: crews who are measured or paid per job skip the LMRA first.\n\nA sharp question: “When did someone last stop a job after the LMRA, and what happened to them?”',
      ],
